221 0 obj
<</E(2.4 Processing Layer)/K[ 5]/P 85 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S/H1/T(2.4 Processing Layer)/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
222 0 obj
<</E(Processing turns raw data into knowledge and runs in two cooperating modes. Batch processing periodically scans the full stored data to build and refresh the base map: imagery is stitched and aligned, machine-learning models read signboards, street names, and house numbers from photographs, and the road network is converted into a weighted graph in which every junction is a node and every road segment is an edge carrying length, speed limit, and turn restrictions.)/K[ 6]/P 85 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
223 0 obj
<</E(Stream processing runs continuously over the live data. Incoming signals are grouped by road segment and short time window, averaged, and compared with the historical pattern for the same segment at the same hour, which produces a current speed and congestion level for every active road. Predictive models then estimate how traffic will change over the next minutes and hours; Google has publicly described using graph neural networks, which treat the road network as a graph, to improve the accuracy of arrival-time estimates. When a user requests directions, a shortest-path algorithm of the Dijkstra or A-star family runs over the graph with the predicted travel times as edge weights and returns candidate routes ranked by duration.)/K[ 7]/P 85 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<</E(Figure 2.2 shows the routing step on a toy network whose edges carry predicted travel times in minutes. The congested segment B-D is expensive, so Dijkstra's algorithm returns the route A-C-E-F-G \(19 minutes\).)/K[ 8]/P 85 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
225 0 obj
<</E(2.3 Data Ingestion and Storage)/K[ 9]/P 85 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S/H1/T(2.3 Data Ingestion and Storage)/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<</E( )/K[ 10]/P 85 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<</E(2.5 Output Layer and Feedback Loop)/K[ 11]/P 85 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S/H1/T(2.5 Output Layer and Feedback Loop)/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<</E( )/K[ 12]/P 85 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
229 0 obj
<</E(The ingestion stage must receive data reliably however it arrives. Slowly changing data such as imagery is loaded in large batches, while live location and incident data is received as continuous streams through messaging and stream-processing services that absorb bursts of traffic, remove duplicates, and attach consistent timestamps. Records are also validated here: impossible speeds are discarded, coordinates are snapped to the nearest plausible road, and personal identifiers are removed. The storage layer then keeps each kind of data in the form that suits how it will be read. Imagery tiles and photographs are kept in distributed file systems that spread every file across many machines and replicate it against hardware failure. Structured records such as places and road segments are kept in distributed wide-column or globally consistent databases that scale horizontally by adding machines, and hot data such as the current speed of a busy highway is held in memory caches for millisecond access. Publicly documented Google technologies such as the Google File System, Colossus, Bigtable, and Spanner are examples of the infrastructure involved.)/K[ 13]/P 85 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
230 0 obj
<</E(The output layer is everythingthe user sees and hears: map tiles at the correct zoom level, the coloured traffic overlay, turn-by-turn guidance, the estimated time of arrival, nearby place suggestions, and re-routing alerts when conditions change. These outputs reach phones, cars, and third-party websites through application programming interfaces and must appear in well under a second, so precomputed tiles and caches are used wherever possible.)/K[ 14]/P 85 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
231 0 obj
<</E(The process map is a loop rather than a straight line. Once a user starts navigating, their phone becomes a new data source, reporting position and speed back to the ingestion stage, while reviews and map corrections flow into the place database and the gap between promised and actual arrival times becomes training signal for the prediction models. Every output therefore becomes an input, a property often called the data network effect. The main challenges noted were location privacy, noisy GPS readings, and uneven accuracy where there are fewer contributors.)/K[ 15]/P 85 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
