284 0 obj
<</E(Feature extraction derives meaningful inputs from the cleaned data, because raw columns are seldom the best predictors. The team engineers features such as the average bill over six months, the change in data usage since the previous quarter, the number of complaints in ninety days, the length of the relationship, and the time left on the contract. A feature selection box then removes correlated or uninformative columns using correlation analysis, model-based importance scores, or principal component analysis, which shortens training and lowers the risk of overfitting. The data is finally split, with class proportions preserved, into a training portion of about eighty per cent and a held-out test portion that stays untouched until the end; scaling statistics are computed on the training portion only, so that no information leaks from the test data.)/K[ 21]/P 87 0 R /Pg 17 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<</E(Algorithm selection is drawn as a decision, because the choice depends on the type and size of the data, the need for interpretability, and the available computing power. For tabular binary classification the candidates are logistic regression, a fast and interpretable baseline; decision trees, which give readable rules; random forests, which average many trees to reduce variance; gradient-boosted trees, which often achieve the best accuracy on structured data; and support vector machines or small neural networks for strongly non-linear patterns. Table 3.1 summarises the trade-offs.)/K[ 22]/P 87 0 R /Pg 17 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
