304 0 obj
<</E(Evaluation also inspects feature importance and error cases, and compares the result with the baseline and the success criteria written at the start. The final decision diamond asks whether those criteria are met. If they are, the model is packaged behind an application programming interface for deployment and monitored for data drift so that it can be retrained when customer behaviour changes; if not, an arrow returns to data collection or feature extraction, showing that machine learning is an iterative cycle rather than a one-way pipeline.)/K[ 15]/P 88 0 R /Pg 18 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
305 0 obj
<</E(In training,the chosen algorithms is fitted to the training data: it searches for the parameters, such as regression coefficients or tree split points, that minimise a loss function measuring the gap between predictions and true labels. Hyperparameters such as tree depth, the number of trees, or the learning rate are set by the designer, and to choose them without touching the test set the team uses k-fold cross-validation, in which the training data is divided into k parts and the model is trained k times, each time validating on a different part.)/K[ 16]/P 88 0 R /Pg 18 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
306 0 obj
<</E(The flowchart contains a loop at this point. If both training and validation scores are low, the model is underfitting, and the team returns to feature engineering or picks a more expressive algorithm. If the training score is high but the validation score is much lower, the model is overfitting, and the team adds regularisation, simplifies the model, collects more data, or removes features. Only when validation performance is acceptable does the arrow leave the loop and continue to the testing stage. Figure 3.2\(a\) shows the typical pattern: training error keeps falling as the model grows more complex, while validation error falls and then rises again.)/K[ 17]/P 88 0 R /Pg 18 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
307 0 obj
<</E(Testingis the single, final measurement of the chosen model on the held-out data, which simulates the unseen customers it will meet in production. Predictions are summarised in a confusion matrix of true positives, false positives, true negatives, and false negatives, from which the team calculates accuracy, precision, recall, the F1-score, and the area under the ROC curve. Accuracy alone is misleading for churn, because a model that predicts that nobody leaves would still be ninety per cent accurate if only ten per cent of customers churn. Recall, the share of real churners caught, and precision, the share of flagged customers who would really have left, are more informative, and the balance between them is set by comparing the cost of a missed churner with the cost of a wasted retention offer.)/K[ 18]/P 88 0 R /Pg 18 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
