353 0 obj
<</E(Three lessons emerged. First, the hidden-layer deltas are roughly an order of magnitude smaller than the output delta, because every step backwards multiplies the signal by a sigmoid slope that can never exceed 0.25; in deep networks this repeated shrinking causes the vanishing gradient problem, one reason modern networks prefer ReLU activations, careful initialisation, normalisation layers, and residual connections. Second, the learning rate sets the step size: too small and training is slow, too large and the updates overshoot the minimum and the loss may oscillate or diverge. Third, backpropagation is efficient because each error signal is computed once and reused, so the backward pass costs about the same as the forward pass, which is what allows networks with billions of parameters to be trained. In practice, examples are processed in mini-batches whose gradients are averaged before each update, and optimisers such as momentum and Adam refine the plain gradient-descent step used here. Frameworks such as PyTorch and TensorFlow automate the process through automatic differentiation, but the manual walkthrough remains valuable for debugging and for understanding new architectures.)/K[ 16]/P 90 0 R /Pg 20 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<</E(Figure 4.2 extends the experiment: repeating the same update for 300 iterations on this single example drives the loss from 0.0372 to about 0.0008, and the sigmoid plot shows why the signal shrinks at every layer.)/K[ 17]/P 90 0 R /Pg 20 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
