434 0 obj
<</E(5.3 Evaluating Clarity and Tone)/K[ 27]/P 93 0 R /Pg 23 0 R /S/H1/T(5.3 Evaluating Clarity and Tone)/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
435 0 obj
<</E(Clarity measures how easily the intended audience understands the message on first reading or hearing. Readability formulas such as the Flesch Reading Ease score estimate difficulty, and qualitative checks ask whether the main message can be stated in one sentence, whether each paragraph holds one idea, and whether the text is free of ambiguity and filler. Factual accuracy is verified separately against reliable sources, because generative models can produce confident but invented statements, known as hallucinations, which make an otherwise clear text misleading.)/K[ 28]/P 93 0 R /Pg 23 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
436 0 obj
<</E(Tone is the attitude a text conveys, such as formal, friendly, persuasive, or empathetic. Evaluators compare the tone requested in the prompt with the tone readers actually perceive, examine word choice and sentence rhythm, and check consistency from start to finish. Sentiment classifiers give a rough indication, but human judgement remains the final authority, because sarcasm and cultural context are hard for software to detect. A tone that suits a social media caption can be unsuitable in an official letter, which is why tone must be judged against the audience and purpose.)/K[ 29]/P 93 0 R /Pg 23 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
437 0 obj
<</E(5.4 Evaluating Body-Language Cues in Multimedia)/K[ 30]/P 93 0 R /Pg 23 0 R /S/H1/T(5.4 Evaluating Body-Language Cues in Multimedia)/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
438 0 obj
<</E(5.5 Findings, Ethics and Conclusion)/K[ 31]/P 93 0 R /Pg 23 0 R /S/H1/T(5.5 Findings, Ethics and Conclusion)/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
439 0 obj
<</E( )/K[ 32]/P 93 0 R /Pg 23 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
440 0 obj
<</E( )/K[ 33]/P 93 0 R /Pg 23 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
441 0 obj
<</E(When the same text is delivered through video,a presentation, oran avatar, meaning is carried by non-verbal signals as well as words, and an excellent script can be undermined by poor delivery. The rubric therefore examines facial expressions, which should match the emotional content of the words; gaze and eye contact, which should be directed at the audience or camera; gestures, which should emphasise key points and stay synchronised with speech rather than looking repetitive or mechanical; posture and head movement, which signal confidence; and vocal delivery, including pace, pauses, pitch, and emphasis. Lip synchronisation is also checked, since small mismatches create the uncanny valley effect, in which an almost-human figure appears unsettling.)/K[ 34]/P 93 0 R /Pg 23 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
442 0 obj
<</E<>/K[ 35]/P 93 0 R /Pg 23 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
443 0 obj
<</E(Combining the three dimensions gives a balanced scorecard in which clarity, tone, and delivery are reported separately and then weighted according to the purpose of the content. Generative tools are strongest at producing well-structured first drafts and adapting a message to different audiences, but they need human oversight for accuracy, originality, and cultural sensitivity. Ethical practice also forms part of the evaluation: AI assistance should be disclosed where appropriate, confidential data should never be pasted into public tools, outputs should be checked for bias and unintended plagiarism, and synthetic voices or faces must never be used to impersonate real people. Evaluating AI-generated content is thus a structured, repeatable process rather than a matter of impression.)/K[ 36]/P 93 0 R /Pg 23 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
444 0 obj
<</E(Because the weighting depends on the purpose of the content, a web article leans on clarity while an avatar video leans on delivery. Table 5.3 gives one example of how a team might set the weights; the percentages are suggestions, not measured results.)/K[ 37]/P 93 0 R /Pg 23 0 R /S/P/Type/StructElem>>
endobj
