1248 0 obj
<</Dest[ 14 0 R /Fit]/Next 1235 0 R /Parent 1223 0 R /Prev 1234 0 R /Title(The group selected Google Maps as its case study and organised the data flow under four headings: data sources, storage, processing, and output. It combines slowly changing planet-scale map data with live traffic data that changes every few seconds. The map presented here is a simplified, educational model built from publicly available descriptions; the internal architecture of a commercial product is proprietary, so the stages should be read as a representative pipeline rather than an official blueprint.)>>
endobj
1249 0 obj
<</Dest[ 14 0 R /Fit]/Next 1250 0 R /Parent 1236 0 R /Title(The first layer contains the places where data originates. The base map is assembled from satellite and aerial imagery, from street-level images captured by camera-equipped vehicles, and from authoritative datasets supplied by governments and mapping agencies, such as road networks, boundaries, and address records. Business and place details come from owner-submitted listings and from user contributions including reviews, photographs, and corrections, while public transport timetables arrive as structured feeds from transit agencies.)>>
endobj
1250 0 obj
<</Dest[ 14 0 R /Fit]/Parent 1236 0 R /Prev 1249 0 R /Title(The most dynamic source is the stream of location signals from the phones of travellers who have chosen to share them. Each signal holds a position, a timestamp, and a speed estimate, and when millions of such records are aggregated and anonymised they reveal how fast traffic is moving on every road segment at that moment. Together these sources show the variety dimension of big data: images, geometries, tables, text, and telemetry at once.)>>
endobj
1251 0 obj
<</Count -2/Dest[ 15 0 R /Fit]/First 1261 0 R /Last 1262 0 R /Next 1252 0 R /Parent 1237 0 R /Title(Dept. Of AI & ML, SUIET, Mukka)>>
endobj
