1291 0 obj
<</Dest[ 16 0 R /Fit]/Next 1292 0 R /Parent 1286 0 R /Title(The first working stage is data collection. For the churn example the team gathers billing records, call and data-usage logs, support tickets, and contract details, and joins them on a customer identifier so that each row describes one subscriber and carries a label showing whether that subscriber left. At this point the team asks whether the data is large enough, representative of future customers, and legally permitted for this use.)>>
endobj
1292 0 obj
<</Count -1/Dest[ 16 0 R /Fit]/First 1299 0 R /Last 1299 0 R /Parent 1286 0 R /Prev 1291 0 R /Title(Raw data is rarely ready for learning, so the next box is cleaning and preprocessing. Missing values are removed or filled with the median, the most frequent category, or a predicted value; duplicates are dropped; impossible entries such as negative call durations are corrected; and outliers are examined rather than deleted blindly. Categorical fields such as contract type are converted to numbers by one-hot encoding, and numeric fields are standardised so that columns with large units do not dominate.)>>
endobj
